WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:02.632
Security tokens are often bearer-like values.

2
00:00:02.632 --> 00:00:12.504
Whoever presents the value within the intended rules may be able to reset
a password, verify an email address, join a workspace, access an API,
submit a form, or continue a session.

3
00:00:12.504 --> 00:00:19.809
That means token generation must consider unpredictability, uniqueness,
lifetime, storage, revocation, and logging together.

4
00:00:19.809 --> 00:00:23.231
A token is not just a random string; it is temporary authority.

5
00:00:23.231 --> 00:00:32.511
Password reset tokens, email verification tokens, session identifiers,
CSRF tokens at a high level, invitation links, and API keys all have
different risk profiles.

6
00:00:32.511 --> 00:00:34.090
Some should be one-time use.

7
00:00:34.090 --> 00:00:35.670
Some need short expiration.

8
00:00:35.670 --> 00:00:36.920
Some need revocation.

9
00:00:36.920 --> 00:00:42.909
Some should be stored only as a verifier or hash so database exposure does
not reveal active tokens directly.

10
00:00:42.909 --> 00:00:46.660
The generation method should match the token's power and lifetime.

11
00:00:46.660 --> 00:00:52.583
Length and encoding matter because they affect guessing resistance,
usability, storage, and transport.

12
00:00:52.583 --> 00:00:55.347
URL-safe encodings may be appropriate for links.

13
00:00:55.347 --> 00:01:05.614
Human-entered codes may need a smaller character set, but shorter user-
friendly codes usually need stronger lifecycle controls, such as
expiration, throttling, and attempt limits.

14
00:01:05.614 --> 00:01:10.286
The right answer depends on how the token is used and how much harm misuse
could cause.

15
00:01:10.286 --> 00:01:12.984
Token presence does not replace authorization.

16
00:01:12.984 --> 00:01:20.618
A valid reset token may allow a password reset, but it should not
automatically grant full account access before the workflow is complete.

17
00:01:20.618 --> 00:01:27.923
A valid session identifier authenticates session state, but the
application still needs authorization for protected resources.

18
00:01:27.923 --> 00:01:33.320
Secure tokens provide a trustworthy handle for a workflow; they do not
remove the need for policy.
